# Avrana `.avrgame` developer tools: experimental export ({{TOOLS_VERSION}})

**EXPERIMENTAL. Not an SDK, not "v0", not stable, not frozen.** The `.avrgame` package format, these
tools and every limit below may change or disappear without notice. There is **no compatibility
promise** and **no signing**: nothing in a package is signed, and nothing here checks who made it.
**A package that passes `validate` is well-formed, not trusted.** Validation reads bytes and checks
data; it says nothing about whether the game is safe, honest or good, and the publisher and licence
fields in a manifest are claims that nobody verified.

## What this is

The validator and packer that the Avrana Party project uses for `.avrgame` files, taken out of the
project's (private) repository so that you can use them without a checkout:

```text
python -m avrana.avrgame validate <file.avrgame | directory-with-avrgame.build.json | recipe.json>
python -m avrana.avrgame pack <path/to/avrgame.build.json> [--out FILE] [--deflate]
python -m avrana.avrgame inspect <file.avrgame> [--json]
python -m avrana.avrgame extract <file.avrgame> <new-or-empty-dir>
```

Standard library only; no network, no installation, no root. It never imports, runs or evaluates
anything from a package. A `.avrgame` is one ZIP file holding a native Avrana game (its Python server
process and its phone-browser client); see `AVRGAME-PACKAGE-FORMAT.md` in this export for the format.

This export is built from the project's source by a script and is reproducible: `MANIFEST.json` lists
every file with its sha256 and the source commit it came from.

## Requirements

- **A stock Python 3.** Verified on **Python 3.12** only (Windows, and Linux in the project's CI). The
  shipped code parses with the Python 3.10 grammar (checked when the export is built), so 3.10 is the
  floor **by inspection**; 3.10 and 3.11 have **not** been run. Newer than 3.12 is untested.
- The commands say `python`; on Linux and macOS use `python3`. macOS is expected to work and is untested.
- Nothing else: no pip, no packages, no network, no appliance hardware, no Party running.

## Unpack and check

```text
sha256sum -c {{NAME}}.zip.sha256          # PowerShell: Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 {{NAME}}.zip
python -m zipfile -e {{NAME}}.zip .
cd {{NAME}}
python -m avrana.avrgame --help
```

The tools find the `avrana` package next to your working directory, so either run them from this
directory, or point `PYTHONPATH` at it and run them from anywhere:

```text
PYTHONPATH=/path/to/{{NAME}} python3 -m avrana.avrgame inspect game.avrgame
# PowerShell:  $env:PYTHONPATH = "C:\path\to\{{NAME}}"; python -m avrana.avrgame inspect game.avrgame
```

The tools read `contracts/capabilities.v0.json` (the capability names; see below) from the `contracts/`
directory beside `avrana/`; keep the two together.

## Using it with the Games developer kit bundle

You need the Games repository's bundle `avrana-gamekit-experimental.1.zip` (the kit, a scaffold, a
conformance run and the Hello Party example). It does not contain the packer; this export does. Unpack
both **side by side**:

```text
work/
  avrana-gamekit-experimental.1/            the kit bundle, unpacked
  {{NAME}}/   this export, unpacked
```

From the **kit** directory (`cd work/avrana-gamekit-experimental.1`), write and pre-flight a game, then
pack it and run the kit's conformance on the packed file:

```text
python -m avrana_gamekit new sparkle sparkle --title "Sparkle Time"
python -m avrana_gamekit check sparkle

PYTHONPATH=../{{NAME}} python3 -m avrana.avrgame validate sparkle/avrgame.build.json
PYTHONPATH=../{{NAME}} python3 -m avrana.avrgame pack sparkle/avrgame.build.json --out out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame
PYTHONPATH=../{{NAME}} python3 -m avrana.avrgame inspect out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame

python -m avrana_gamekit.conformance out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame
```

In PowerShell, set the variable for the three packer commands and remove it afterwards (the
conformance run starts your game as the appliance would and should not have the tools on its path):

```text
$env:PYTHONPATH = "$PWD\..\{{NAME}}"
python -m avrana.avrgame validate sparkle/avrgame.build.json
python -m avrana.avrgame pack sparkle/avrgame.build.json --out out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame
python -m avrana.avrgame inspect out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame
Remove-Item Env:PYTHONPATH
python -m avrana_gamekit.conformance out/sparkle-0.1.0.avrgame
```

`validate` prints the id, version, file count, size and the archive's sha256; `pack` prints the file it
wrote and the same sha256; `inspect` also prints the manifest summary and every file (publisher and
licence marked as claims). The sha256 is identical for the same inputs on every machine, because `pack`
sorts the entries, fixes the timestamps and modes and stores the files uncompressed.

**A package can validate and still fail to start.** Validation checks bytes and that the `-m` module
exists inside the package; it does not run your game. The gate that does is the kit's conformance run on
the packed file (the last command above). If it fails, fix the game or the build recipe (the usual cause
is a module the game imports that the recipe does not include) and pack again.

The kit's own README and walkthrough describe writing and testing the game itself.

## The build recipe

`pack` and `validate` take a **build recipe**, `avrgame.build.json`, which the kit generates for you:

```json
{"recipe": "{{RECIPE_FORMAT}}", "manifest": "avrgame.json", "root": "..", "include": ["sparkle", "..."]}
```

`manifest` and `root` are relative to the recipe file (defaults `avrgame.json` and `.`); each `include`
entry is relative to `root`, may not leave it, and directories are included recursively. Unknown keys
are refused. The recipe is a convenience for the packer and is **not** inside the `.avrgame`.

## Limits (experimental values)

| Limit | Value |
|---|---|
| Format string, exactly | `{{FORMAT}}` |
| Files (entries) in a package | {{MAX_FILES}} |
| One file, unpacked | {{MAX_FILE_MIB}} MiB |
| All files, unpacked | {{MAX_TOTAL_MIB}} MiB |
| The archive file | {{MAX_ARCHIVE_MIB}} MiB |
| `avrgame.json` | {{MAX_MANIFEST_KIB}} KiB |
| A file name | {{MAX_NAME_LENGTH}} characters, {{MAX_DEPTH}} path segments |
| `server.args` | 1 to {{MAX_ARGS}} strings, each 1 to {{MAX_ARG}} printable characters |

Sizes are checked against the bytes actually read, not only the sizes an archive claims.

## What is refused, and why

Validation lists **every** problem it finds, one per line on stderr, and exits 1. Exit 0 is success and
2 is a usage error.

- **File names**: relative, `/` separated, ASCII letters, digits and `. _ + @ = ~ -` only; no empty, `.`
  or `..` segment, no backslash, drive letter, `:` or control character, no dotfile, no segment ending in
  `.` or a space, no Windows device name (`CON`, `NUL`, `COM1` ...), no two names differing only by case.
  (So the package unpacks the same on every platform and cannot write outside its own directory.)
- **Key material**: any file named `*.key`, `*.pem`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `id_rsa*`, `*.env` or `.env`.
  Secrets must never travel in a package; `pack` refuses it as well as the validator.
- **Not plain files**: symbolic links, devices, FIFOs, sockets, setuid/setgid/sticky bits, encrypted
  entries and compression other than stored or deflate. **Python bytecode** (`*.pyc`, `__pycache__`),
  because bytecode cannot be reviewed as source. Inside a directory the packer skips hidden files,
  `__pycache__`, `*.pyc` and `avrgame.build.json` by itself.
- **The manifest** (`avrgame.json`, strict JSON: duplicate keys, `NaN`, a byte-order mark or an unknown key
  anywhere is an error): `format` must be exactly the string above, so a format this tool does not know
  is refused rather than guessed at.
- **Appliance-owned names**: the manifest may not carry a tier, entry path, health check, granted
  permission, key, socket, command, environment or absolute path. A package **requests**; the
  appliance owner **decides**. These are refused by name.
- **Reserved**: top-level `signature`, `provenance`, `entitlement` and `publisher_key` are refused as
  "not implemented", so an ignored signature can never look like a checked one.
- **Which games can be packaged**: only a `native` game with `runtime.type` `external` and `start`
  `service`. Emulated, LAN-module and arcade games are not packageable. The game's own contract must be
  a complete `avrana.game/v0` Game Contract; `game.package` is not allowed (use the manifest's `package`
  block); `game.id` is the package id.
- **`server.interpreter`** is a **name** from the appliance's list, today only `python3`, never a path.
  `server.args` for `python3` must be exactly `["-m", "<dotted module>", ...]`, and the module must
  exist in the package as `NAME/__main__.py` or `NAME.py` (checked by reading the file list; nothing
  is imported). `-c`, a script path and other interpreter options are refused. No argument may be an
  absolute path or contain `..`.
- **`client.root`** is a relative directory inside the package and must contain `index.html`.
- **Capability names**: the Game Contract's `requires` and `optional` lists may use only names from the
  capability vocabulary, listed with a one-line meaning in the section "Capability names" below. The file
  `contracts/capabilities.v0.json` in this export holds the same names; its labels, summaries and probe
  kinds are placeholders, because the tools never read them. The validator's message for any other name
  says only that the name is unknown; it does not list the valid ones, so use the table.

### Reading the refusal messages

The messages are the project's own, unchanged, so some say "this appliance" (for example "this appliance
implements [...], not ..."). In these tools that means **these tools**: the value your package asks for is
not one that this export of the tools speaks. The usual fix is to build your game for the versions in the
table below, or, if the game is newer than the tools, to ask whoever gave you this export for a newer one.

## Capability names

A presentation in the Game Contract says what a phone, or the appliance, must be able to do for that
presentation to work, by naming capabilities in `requires` (and `optional`). These are all the names
the validator accepts, and what a game is saying when it lists one. The first group are features of the
phone's browser (named after the Web platform feature of the same name); the second group are
things the appliance may have available, and matter only to a presentation whose picture or input does not
come from the phone's browser alone. **A `browser_native` presentation needs none of the `runtime.*`, `input.*` or
`presentation.*` names.**

| Name | What a game is saying by requiring it |
|---|---|
| `secure_context` | The page is served over HTTPS (or from localhost), which the browser demands before it offers several other features. |
| `service_worker` | The browser can register a service worker (a background script that can cache the game for offline use). |
| `cache_storage` | The browser offers the Cache Storage API for keeping downloaded files. |
| `websocket` | The browser can open a WebSocket, a two-way connection to the game's server. |
| `webrtc` | The browser supports WebRTC peer connections (live audio, video and data between endpoints). |
| `webrtc.data_channel` | The browser can open a WebRTC data channel (low-latency messages without a server in between). |
| `video.h264` | The browser can play video compressed with the common AVC codec. |
| `web_audio` | The browser offers the Web Audio API for generating and mixing sound. |
| `gamepad` | The browser has the Gamepad API (this does not mean that a controller is connected). |
| `wake_lock` | The browser can keep the screen from switching off (Screen Wake Lock API). |
| `web_crypto` | The browser offers the Web Crypto API (hashing, random values and encryption in the page). |
| `storage.local` | The browser offers `localStorage` for small values kept on the device. |
| `storage.indexeddb` | The browser offers IndexedDB for larger structured data kept on the device. |
| `storage.persistent` | The browser has already marked this site's storage as persistent (the page does not ask for it). |
| `webgl` | The browser can draw 3D graphics with WebGL. |
| `webgl2` | The browser can draw 3D graphics with WebGL 2. |
| `webgpu` | The browser can draw and compute on the graphics chip with WebGPU. |
| `fullscreen` | The browser lets the page fill the whole screen (Fullscreen API). |
| `vibration` | The browser can make the phone vibrate (Vibration API). |
| `media_devices` | The browser offers the call that opens cameras and microphones (the check opens nothing). |
| `microphone` | The player has already granted the page permission to use the microphone. |
| `camera` | The player has already granted the page permission to use the camera. |
| `touch` | The device has a touch screen. |
| `runtime.lan_games` | The appliance has its older built-in game server installed. This describes the appliance, not your game. |
| `runtime.retroarch` | The appliance has its emulator runtime available. This describes the appliance, not your game. |
| `input.virtual_gamepad` | The appliance can turn phone input into a virtual game controller for each player. |
| `presentation.shared_stream` | The appliance can send one video stream of the whole picture to every phone. |
| `presentation.personal_viewport.crop` | The appliance can give each player a cut-out of one shared picture. |
| `presentation.personal_viewport.dedicated_stream` | The appliance can render and send each player a video stream of their own. |
| `presentation.tv` | The appliance can show the game on a shared screen or television, while phones act as controllers. |

## Permissions

`game.runtime.permissions` in the Game Contract is a **request**. The names this version knows are:
{{PERMISSIONS}}. An unknown name is refused. Whether any of them is granted is the appliance owner's
decision, per install; a package cannot grant itself anything, and validation grants nothing. The
experimental installer today additionally requires a package to request `party_roster` (the roster of
the session); the kit's scaffold does.

## What `requires` means

`requires` states which protocol versions the game was built for, and **all three are mandatory**:

| Key | Value this export accepts |
|---|---|
| `session` | `{{REQ_SESSION}}` |
| `bridge` | `{{REQ_BRIDGE}}` |
| `result` | `{{REQ_RESULT}}` |

A value the Party does not implement is refused ("compatibility fails closed"), so a package built for
another version is stopped at validation rather than at the party. The kit writes the right values.

## What your game will run under on an appliance

<!-- Sources, not shipped (checked 2026-10-12 against this tree):
     deploy/games/avrana-game@.service and avrana-game@.socket (RestrictAddressFamilies, Restart, DynamicUser,
     LoadCredential, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE); avrana/ops/provision_game.py COMMUNITY_UNIT (the ceilings, read at
     build time; PrivateNetwork, CapabilityBoundingSet) and NO_STATE; docs/design/AVRGAME-PACKAGE.md "What an
     installed package can and cannot do" and "party_roster is mandatory"; avrana/ops/install_game.py (grant
     rules); ADR 0016 section 4 (socket activation, stops itself when idle). -->

These are the conditions the project's installer and unit templates set up **in code**. They are
**experimental and not deployed**: they were exercised on a throwaway test machine in the project's CI,
never on a real appliance, and **this export does not verify any of it**. Treat them as what to design
for, not as a promise.

- **One way to start.** `python3 -m <module>`, from the package root, with the appliance's system Python
  and the **standard library only**. No other interpreter, no script path, no installing packages.
- **No IP networking at all.** The process may use Unix sockets only (it cannot open a TCP or UDP socket,
  and runs in an empty network namespace). It is **socket-activated**: the platform
  creates the listening socket and starts your process on the first connection, handing the socket over as
  file descriptor 3 (the kit's runtime does this for you). Your server must accept that socket and not bind
  its own port.
- **No network at install or play.** The installer reads the `.avrgame` file it is given and fetches
  nothing; your game cannot fetch anything either. Ship everything it needs inside the package.
- **The package is read-only by ownership.** The working directory is the unpacked package: its files are
  owned by root and your game runs as another user, so it cannot change them. There is no separate
  filesystem sandbox directive, so your game can still read other files on the machine that anyone may read.
  Python bytecode is not written. `/tmp` is private to the running game and is discarded when it stops.
- **No persistent state unless granted.** A directory that survives restarts exists only when the
  appliance owner **granted** `persistent_storage`; without it there is none and no `$STATE_DIRECTORY`.
  Granted storage has no quota. Keep state you cannot lose small and recoverable.
- **Resource ceilings (provisional, may change).** At present a package's process gets a memory limit of
  {{MEMORY_MAX}}, at most {{TASKS_MAX}} tasks (threads and processes) and {{CPU_QUOTA}} of one CPU; it also
  runs with no extra privileges (an empty capability set). There is no system-call filter and no disk quota
  yet. These values are conservative guesses, not measured on a real game or on real appliance hardware, and going over them stops **your
  process**, not the appliance.
- **`party_roster` is mandatory in this experimental format.** The Party hands every game the roster of its
  session at launch (each participant's id, display name and role), so the installer refuses a package that
  does not request `party_roster` and an install that does not grant it. What the permission should cover
  is still open and may change.
- **Grants are recorded; few are enforced.** The owner decides which requested permissions to grant, and
  the grant is written in a record next to the install. The platform does **not** check granted
  permissions when a session starts: of them only `persistent_storage` is enforced (by the unit, as above).
  For that reason the installer refuses to grant any permission it cannot enforce, which today is every
  permission except `persistent_storage` and `party_roster`. A package may validate while asking for a
  permission (such as `camera` or `internet`) that no install can be granted today.
- **The platform starts, restarts and stops your process.** It is started on demand, restarted if it
  exits with a failure, and is expected to **stop itself when idle** (no session, no report pending) and
  on a termination signal; the kit's runtime does both. A game that never exits holds its memory.
- **Your game is not trusted.** It runs as a throwaway unprivileged identity with its own key; it can
  read world-readable files on the machine and talk to the Party's internal socket with that key only. It
  cannot act as another game.

## What it cannot do

- **It does not install anything.** Installation is the appliance owner's action (`install-game`, run as
  root on the appliance, with the owner's grants). Nothing in this export talks to an appliance, and
  nothing here is deployed anywhere.
- It does not **sign** or **verify the publisher** of a package, and it does not judge whether a game
  is safe. A valid package is untrusted input.
- It does not prove that your game **starts**, only that the package is well-formed. Run the kit's
  conformance on the packed file.
- It is not the kit: it does not scaffold, play or test a game.
- It does not publish or upload the `.avrgame`. Handing the file to the appliance owner is up to you.

## What is in this export

`avrana/avrgame/` (the tools) and `avrana/contracts/` (the Game Contract validator and its strict JSON
reader): the project's own code, **code unchanged**. Comments and docstrings that named internal material
of the project were removed from these copies, and `MANIFEST.json` flags every file where that happened (the
build checks that the code is identical once docstrings are stripped). `avrana/party/`,
`avrana/contracts/party_games.py` and `avrana/__init__.py` are **generated stand-in modules**, each holding
only a version string or a path; the Party's session protocol, result implementation and its
Party-to-Games declaration are **not** in this export. `contracts/` holds the capability names the
validator accepts. Also in this export: this README, `NOTICE.md`, `AVRGAME-PACKAGE-FORMAT.md` (a snapshot of
the format description) and `MANIFEST.json` (every file's sha256 and where it came from, the format the
tools speak and the source commit, marked `source_dirty` when the tree had uncommitted changes).

Read `NOTICE.md` before passing this on: the terms under which these files may be redistributed are an
open owner decision, and it lists what the export reveals about the platform.
